    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Now, flipping it to the social democrat side—if we're trying to be equally rigorous, how would a social democrat respond to that \"self-ownership\" argument? Specifically, do they see the libertarian view of \"absolute\" property rights as a logically sound framework that just happens to have different priorities, or is there a fundamental flaw in their logic that makes it impossible to reconcile with a functioning society? I want to know if their critique is just \"different values\" or if they view the libertarian position as fundamentally irrational or unethical.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Social democrats argue that the libertarian \"self-ownership\" framework is not merely a difference in priority but is fundamentally flawed because it logically preempts any moral obligation to the community, making absolute individual rights incompatible with a cohesive society. They contend that human existence is inherently social and interdependent; therefore, the ideology of \"absolute\" property is an abstraction that ignores the objective reality of systemic inequalities and the collective need for a democratic politics. Consequently, social democrats view the libertarian position not just as one with \"different values,\" but as an unethical stance that, by justifying the appropriation of one person's labor for the whole without consent, actively undermines the very freedom it claims to protect.",
